passages=[
 {'id':'P1','title':'A Working Marsh in the City','subtitle':'An original practice passage inspired by urban ecology, not an IELTS recall','paragraphs':[
 {'label':'A','text':'In 2018, a coastal city converted a disused loading yard beside the Alder Creek into a fourteen-hectare public marsh. The site had once been sealed under concrete, so rain ran quickly into the street drains. Planners wanted a place that could hold some stormwater, provide habitat and give residents access to a landscape that had largely disappeared from the district. They did not describe the project as a substitute for the city’s flood-defence system. Instead, it was intended to be one part of a wider programme that also repaired drains and raised several vulnerable entrances.'},
 {'label':'B','text':'The design team shaped the ground into terraces at three different elevations. The lowest terrace is frequently reached by brackish water, the middle terrace is wet after storms, and the upper terrace remains relatively dry. Each zone supports different plants. Sensors record water depth and salt levels, helping the gardeners decide where young plants are likely to survive. This arrangement matters because a single flat basin would provide fewer conditions for wildlife and would be more difficult to maintain after a very high tide.'},
 {'label':'C','text':'Some residents initially worried that the marsh would produce mosquitoes. The project team therefore avoided isolated pools: channels allow water to move in and out with the tides twice a day. A survey found fewer mosquito larvae at the site after restoration than in the abandoned yard’s blocked drains. Dragonflies and small fish also feed on larvae. The managers decided against broad pesticide spraying because it could harm those predators, although they continue to inspect the channels after storms.'},
 {'label':'D','text':'A raised boardwalk lets visitors cross the central part without stepping on plants. Local teachers used it for thirty-two class visits in the first full school year. A smaller path near nesting birds is closed from April to July, while other viewing areas remain available. Signs ask visitors to stay on the boards and explain why even a short step into the reeds can damage new shoots. Volunteers lead occasional walks, but trained staff still inspect the structures and water controls.'},
 {'label':'E','text':'During a major storm in 2022, a gauge on a street beside the marsh recorded water eighteen centimetres below the level measured at a comparable upstream street. The result attracted attention, but the engineers cautioned against treating it as proof that the marsh alone prevented flooding. Drainage pipes were improved in the same year, rainfall varied across the neighbourhood, and the two gauges were not a controlled experiment. The data support further monitoring rather than a simple claim of cause and effect.'},
 {'label':'F','text':'Wildlife surveys offer a similarly careful story. Observers recorded seven fish species in the creek in 2023, compared with three before construction. The wider river was also becoming cleaner over this period, so the increase cannot confidently be assigned to the new marsh alone. The team now compares data with other creeks that did not receive a restoration project. Such comparisons are slower than a celebratory headline, but they make the findings more useful to future designers.'},
 {'label':'G','text':'The largest long-term question is maintenance. Construction was funded by a one-off capital grant, while litter removal, sensor replacement and boardwalk repairs require money every year. A neighbourhood group helps with planting days and observations, yet it cannot replace the engineers responsible for flood controls. The council has set aside a small recurring budget and will review it annually. The marsh is therefore best understood as living infrastructure: its value depends on continued care as well as its initial design.'}
 ]},
 {'id':'P2','title':'How a Port Learned to Keep Time','subtitle':'An original practice passage about changing time signals','paragraphs':[
 {'label':'A','text':'For most of the eighteenth century, people in the harbour town of Bellport did not expect every clock to agree. Church bells, the sun and a noon gun provided public cues, but these were sufficient mainly for local routines. A merchant could arrange to meet after the market bell without needing a minute-by-minute schedule. Ships were different: navigators needed a reliable time reference to estimate longitude. Their need for precision was one reason that new instruments began to enter port life.'},
 {'label':'B','text':'Marine chronometers offered time that could travel with a vessel. Their early versions were expensive and demanded careful handling; a shock or neglected winding could undermine a reading. A captain compared the chronometer with a known local signal before departure and checked it again whenever possible. The instrument did not eliminate the need for public time. Rather, it made the quality of that public reference more important to a growing number of sailors.'},
 {'label':'C','text':'In 1836, the Bellport observatory installed a large time ball on its roof. At one o’clock in the afternoon, an assistant dropped the ball so that crews in the harbour could compare their chronometers at the same visible moment. A flag had been tried earlier, but its movement was harder to distinguish against a busy skyline. The ball worked well in clear weather; fog or heavy rain could hide it completely. The observatory kept a log of missed signals and posted corrections at the quay.'},
 {'label':'D','text':'The arrival of the telegraph allowed time measured at a central observatory to reach many towns almost instantly. Railway companies pressed for a shared standard because a timetable written in several local solar times could confuse passengers and staff. Some Bellport shopkeepers objected, arguing that the new clock time no longer matched the sun above their own town. Others welcomed the predictability. The change was negotiated gradually, with public clocks and railway notices displaying both systems for several years.'},
 {'label':'E','text':'A municipal clock installed in 1896 was more than a practical device. Its four illuminated faces could be seen from the docks, the station and the market. Officials promised weekly inspection and displayed a notice whenever the mechanism required repair. Newspapers treated a stopped clock as evidence that the council had broken a public promise. The clock became a visible statement that common time belonged to everyone, even though the machinery behind that promise was still imperfect.'},
 {'label':'F','text':'Radio signals spread in the following century and gave clockmakers another way to check their work. They did not make every street clock unnecessary: people still needed an easily visible display, especially where radio receivers were rare or costly. The signal also had to be interpreted correctly by the equipment receiving it. Accurate timekeeping was thus a chain of measurements and maintenance, not a single invention that solved every earlier problem.'},
 {'label':'G','text':'Today, phones often obtain time from network services linked to satellite systems. This can make an ordinary display remarkably accurate, but a port still keeps independent references for safety. A failed network connection should not leave a rescue crew unable to coordinate its logs. Bellport’s old time ball is now operated for visitors once a week. It reminds them that a trusted clock depends on people, agreed standards and backup arrangements as much as on the device in front of them.'}
 ]},
 {'id':'P3','title':'What a Seed Bank Can and Cannot Save','subtitle':'An original practice passage on crop diversity','paragraphs':[
 {'label':'A','text':'A field planted with one highly productive crop variety can yield a great deal under familiar conditions. It can also become vulnerable if a disease or drought affects that variety particularly badly. Seed banks preserve samples of different varieties so that breeders and farmers have more options in the future. This is a form of insurance, but it does not protect a harvest automatically: someone must identify useful traits, grow plants and make seed available in time.'},
 {'label':'B','text':'Many crop seeds are described as orthodox, meaning that they tolerate careful drying and cold storage. Staff first reduce their moisture content, seal them in suitable containers and place them at a low temperature. The method slows deterioration, but does not stop it forever. The bank records the original source of each sample and checks storage conditions continuously. A freezer full of anonymous packets would be far less useful than a smaller collection with clear records.'},
 {'label':'C','text':'Other plants produce seeds that cannot survive the same treatment. These are sometimes called recalcitrant seeds. For such species, a living field collection may be maintained, or tiny pieces of tissue may be preserved using cryopreservation. Both approaches demand specialist care. A field collection can be damaged by the same storm that threatens farmers, while a tissue sample may require laboratory work before it becomes a healthy plant again.'},
 {'label':'D','text':'Stored seeds must be tested for viability. A small sample is taken from a packet and a germination test measures how many seeds still grow. If the rate falls too low, staff plant some seeds to produce a fresh generation. That regeneration step is not neutral: selecting only the fastest-growing plants can cause genetic drift, gradually changing the collection. Managers therefore set rules for sample size and for recording which plants contribute to the new seed.'},
 {'label':'E','text':'Records are as important as the containers. Metadata may include the collection site, climate, farmer’s description and conditions under which a sample may be shared. Some communities require consent before their locally developed varieties are used in research or commercial breeding. A bank that ignores these terms could lose trust even if its scientific storage work is excellent. Sharing seeds responsibly requires legal and social care alongside biological knowledge.'},
 {'label':'F','text':'Consider a fictional farming district called Northfield. After an unusually dry season, its farmers asked for varieties that had previously grown under limited rainfall. The seed bank could identify candidate packets because it had recorded where they came from. However, the packets contained only small quantities. Farmers had to multiply the seed over several growing cycles before it could cover many fields. Storage created an option; local production turned that option into a practical response.'},
 {'label':'G','text':'A seed bank is consequently one part of a broader system. Researchers, extension workers and farmers must test whether stored varieties suit current soil, weather and food needs. Keeping diversity in farmers’ fields also allows plants to continue adapting outside a freezer. The strongest strategy combines safe storage, accurate metadata, respectful access rules and continued cultivation. None of these measures removes uncertainty, but together they leave more choices open when conditions change.'}
 ]}
]
